  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B033",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B014",
    "B036"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are an unusually complete physics case: the magic-wavelength concept makes many trapped atoms available without forfeiting clock accuracy, and subsequent development has delivered a reproducible new regime of precision measurement. The achievement joins a sharply identifiable physical idea to exceptional experiments, with consequences for tests of fundamental physics rather than depending on a prospective technological application. Katori and Ye provide a compact and credible attribution from originating concept through decisive experimental development.",
    "case_against": "A final historical review must distinguish the lattice-clock invention from indispensable precursor work in optical frequency metrology and assess whether the two-name attribution adequately represents the field. The pending institutional redefinition of the second should not itself be treated as evidence of scientific merit, although it does not weaken the demonstrated physics.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that lattice clocks enable measurements of gravity, fundamental constants, and relativistic time, while the SI redefinition remains incomplete.",
        "response": "I agree that these measurements demonstrate a genuinely new precision regime. I would frame the prize around the physical invention and development of lattice clocks, not around an eventual standards decision; the latter is a useful maturity signal but neither necessary nor sufficient for the case."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that the Aharonov-Bohm effect and geometric phases are deeply consequential and experimentally mature, while noting that they are related but not identical discoveries.",
        "response": "That qualification remains important. B014 is an exceptionally strong alternative, but its two historical strands require a more delicate unified citation and attribution rationale than B033. I would prefer to resolve that conceptual boundary before making it the present award."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth identifies trapped-ion quantum logic as a mature, reproducible realization of quantum processing, with Cirac, Zoller, and Blatt spanning theory and experiment.",
        "response": "I share the admiration for the clean control achieved with ions and regard B036 as a serious alternative. Yet its proposed citation risks leaning toward an unfinished computing application; lattice clocks offer a comparably profound quantum-control advance whose fundamental metrological achievement is already fully established."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical scrutiny identifies additional indispensable contributors to the optical-lattice-clock lineage beyond the proposed two laureates.",
      "Whether B014 can be framed as one sufficiently unified achievement without obscuring the distinct histories of the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
